00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.750
So where you sit
in the ecosystem,

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:05.110
be it a care provider,
researcher, supporting staff,

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.390
or health care decision
maker, technology

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:10.090
is changing the way
health care is delivered.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.910
Common priorities for digital
innovation among health care

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.790
systems, hospitals,
and care facilities

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.760
include improving the
wellness of the community

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.990
through improved
access to patient care,

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.970
be it retail clinic,
telehealth, home monitoring,

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.180
or other virtual
health care option.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.610
Reducing the cost
and complexity of IT

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:32.680
and increasing
agility, particularly

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.010
in the face of budget and
funding uncertainties.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.930
Supporting new health
care business models

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.320
by providing anytime, anywhere
access to patient information

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:43.690
via remote and
multi-channel access

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.720
to mobile and web applications.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.220
Strengthening
protection and defense

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.730
of PHI and other
sensitive health details

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.440
by leveraging
information security

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.460
in the face of new and more
sophisticated cyber threats.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.820
Improving the quality of patient
services and business decisions

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:05.190
through more and better use
of big data analytics and IoT.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.780
Four key trends
transforming the industry

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.830
and driving the needs for those
priorities are as follows.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:15.950
The ongoing need to reduce
costs in the delivery of care--

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:17.500
health care systems
around the world

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.280
are struggling to
deliver satisfactory care

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.080
in the face of budget cuts
and increasing demands

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.620
from an aging and growing
global population.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.920
Workplace challenges
contributing to this need

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.634
are the time and
staff shortages.

00:03:33.634 --> 00:03:35.800
As physicians are pressured
to deliver an increasing

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.880
number of preventative services,
follow guidelines, engage

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.320
in evidence-based practice, and
deliver patient-centered care

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.310
in managerially-driven
organizations,

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:46.960
they struggle with
how much control

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.090
they have over their own time.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.500
According to the biannual
physician survey, only 14%

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.350
believe they have the time they
need to provide the highest

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.840
standards of care.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.060
Additionally, the current
worldwide shortage

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.960
of physicians, nurses, and other
health workers is 7 million.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.490
And the gap is widening.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.240
Some doctors are looking
to leave the profession

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.700
amid burnout and low morale
due to regulatory and paperwork

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.100
burdens and a loss
of clinical autonomy,

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:19.050
according to the World
Health Organization.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.820
Training can also be a factor.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.490
The BGL Group reports
that approximately 40%

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.040
of employees who receive
poor job training

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.020
are likely to leave
their positions

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.860
within the first year.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.140
The second trend
transforming the industry

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.950
is the increasing threat of
cyber attacks and data loss.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:39.050
With growing concerns
about cybersecurity

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.690
and the loss of sensitive
information, this is changing.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.420
And the risk of not
overhauling and moving forward

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.730
with new viable
technology solutions

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:48.920
is becoming the greater threat.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.490
The Poneman Institute estimated
an average breach cost

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:57.740
of $3.5 million in 2017
with a 27% probability

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.300
that a US company
would experience

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:05.120
a breach in the next 24 months,
costing between $1.1 and $3.8

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.830
million.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:09.800
If you multiply that
estimate per record cost

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.600
for a breach across
the industry,

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.550
multiple breaches
combined would potentially

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:15.650
cost hundreds of
millions of dollars.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.700
The third key trend driving
transformation in health care

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.930
is higher service expectations
from digitally-savvy patients.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.030
Today's digitally-savvy
patients expect

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:30.850
on-demand personalized service.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:32.680
Digitalization and
the implementation

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:34.840
of mobile strategies
can no longer

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.570
be approached as an afterthought
in the delivery of care

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:40.060
and patient experience.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.570
Mobile is becoming
ubiquitous in health care,

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:45.460
playing a significant role as
the industry continues to adopt

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.750
a patient-centered approach.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:49.215
Health care leaders
are now seeking

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:51.340
to improve the patient
experience through tailoring

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.230
patient engagement
strategies to meet

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:57.100
the expectations of their
digitally-savvy patients.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.260
One example of this
is the proliferation

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:00.880
of mobile devices
in health care,

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:04.150
as it helps to reduce
paperwork time by 60%

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.700
and increase patient
face time by 29%.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.340
And the fourth key trend driving
transformation health care

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.620
is advancing disease
prediction and prevention

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.260
through insights and
machine learning.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.450
From high-end diagnostic
imaging systems

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:22.870
that let doctors catch
deadly diseases like cancer

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:25.360
in their early stages
to voice recognition

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.390
systems in the operating room,
which help doctors get a better

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:31.060
view of parts of the
body while operating,

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:35.290
technology has made life easier
for both doctors and patients.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:39.310
However, although AI is
a hot health care topic,

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:42.460
some haven't felt
ready, meaning it still

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:45.490
needs to be translated
into reality, especially

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:49.470
in an industry as
complex as health care.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.420
Among the challenges
to widespread use

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.580
are the acceptance
of AI applications

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.370
in clinical practice,
difficulty leveraging

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:01.460
divergent personal network
devices and AI solutions,

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:03.040
and access to
quality training data

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.150
on AI applications and health
and gaps in data streams.

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:11.490
As health care moves beyond
the walls of the hospital,

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.300
so health care IT must move
beyond the data center.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:18.180
The need to adopt digital tools
and develop new strategies

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.970
to change the way care
is delivered to patients

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:22.897
and residents may seem obvious.

00:07:22.897 --> 00:07:24.480
But the health care
industry sometimes

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.870
lags behind others in
its use of technology.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.370
It's not that health
care providers

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:32.130
don't want to try new,
innovative tools to support

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:33.150
better models.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:36.660
But the risk of failure has
often been deemed too high.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.240
The implementation of cloud
strategies and digitalization

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.910
can no longer be approached as
an afterthought in the delivery

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.092
of care and patient experience.

00:07:44.092 --> 00:07:46.050
It will not only help
meet the needs of today's

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.940
digitally-savvy patients.

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.970
It also has a potential to
impact every aspect of care

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.950
delivery and
operations, enabling

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:55.830
more efficient
utilization of resources,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.260
more time with patients
at the point of care,

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:02.190
and a more secure and
governed working model.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:05.040
Like it has in other industries
and commercial enterprises,

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.440
cloud-based technology
will play a pivotal role

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.930
in overhauling health and
social care environments,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.360
providing care professionals
with secure, mobile access

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.780
to information and resources
at the point of delivery

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:18.240
and opening up new ways
to collaborate and improve

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.150
the quality of patient care.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.430
In a traditional
computing model,

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:26.940
you would bring data
to the individual.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.310
Data sharing in that
model essentially

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:32.370
implies data copying.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:35.280
Costs can be high
and unpredictable.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:38.940
There are burdens associated
with installs, integrations,

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:40.799
and upgrades.

00:08:40.799 --> 00:08:44.310
Data silos detract from
insights and correlation.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:47.060
And software deployment
can be inflexible.

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:49.020
But in a cloud
computing model, you

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:52.200
bring individuals to the data.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.030
In this model,
simplicity is king,

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.330
and users truly share data.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:59.400
Costs for services
are predictable.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:04.170
The focus is on business value,
not managing infrastructure.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:07.470
There is a comprehensive,
patient-centric view.

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:12.420
And apps and compute
can be done anywhere.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:13.290
Let's go back.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.350
Sorry about that.

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:18.770
Google Cloud offers many tools
within its enterprise portfolio

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:21.995
to address health care's digital
priorities and the trends

00:09:21.995 --> 00:09:24.980
driving digital transformation
that we just discussed.

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:27.140
With more than a
billion users leveraging

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.030
seven of those
products each month,

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:32.510
the solutions range from storage
and compute, machine learning

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:36.380
and artificial intelligence,
productivity and collaboration,

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:39.080
to the platform that
